## Updated AndAssociativity scoreboard (iter 136 — rule-trajectory comparison)

The "thvm 12.1s vs wmcli 10s = 2s engine gap" framing in iter 133 was wrong.
It compared thvm KBO+Gt to wmcli LPO, which find different proofs.  Honest
per-rule throughput at the SAME algorithm:

| Path                                              | Rules | Wall  | Rules/s | Notes                          |
|---------------------------------------------------|-------|-------|---------|---------------------------------|
| `FindEquationalProof` (Mathematica built-in)      | ~1673 | 7.4 s | ~226    | private build; LPO+Mix          |
| `wmcli andassoc.pr` (LTO+m1)                      |  1673 | 10.4s |  161    | WM LPO, p>q>r>nand              |
| thvm KBO + Gt + FLATTERM (current default)        |   338 | 12.8s |   26    | DIFFERENT trajectory, shorter   |
| thvm LPO + Mix + Wald + nand-highest precedence   |   199 | 30.5s |  6.5    | TIMEOUT, matches WM algo        |
| thvm LPO + Mix + Wald + skolems-highest (WM-prec) |   198 | 30.2s |  6.6    | TIMEOUT, matches WM precedence  |

**Two distinct gaps, not one:**

1. **Trajectory gap**: thvm's KBO+Gt finds a 338-rule proof (5x shorter than
   WM's 1673).  This is a different completion, not a faster algorithm.  In
   matched-algorithm mode (LPO+Mix+Wald) thvm can't even reach a proof.

2. **Per-rule cost gap**: at matched LPO algorithm, thvm is 24x slower per
   rule than wmcli (6.6 r/s vs 161 r/s).  Precedence direction (nand-high
   vs skolems-high) makes no measurable difference (199 vs 198 rules at
   30s).  The 24x is the real engine speed gap.

The KBO+Gt "win" (12.8s vs WM 10s) hides this: a shortcut to a shorter
proof masks a 24x per-rule cost gap.  When the user asks "is the engine
slower?" the honest answer is YES, by ~24x on matched algorithm — KBO+Gt
just happens to crack AndAssoc in fewer steps so the wall comes out close.

### What this means for the algorithmic gap

WM at 161 rules/s and thvm at 26 rules/s (KBO+Gt path) have similar per-
rule cost.  thvm at 6.6 rules/s on the WM-matched LPO+Mix+Wald path is 4x
slower than its own KBO+Gt path — thvm's LPO compare is significantly
slower than its KBO compare, AND its Mix+Wald CP-selection is doing more
per-iteration bookkeeping than Gt.

The "atp_rewrite_normalize hot path" call chain identified in iter 134
(atp_ft_unorient_step -> thvm_kbo, 938+614+314 samples) was profiled
under KBO+Gt+FLATTERM.  Under LPO+Mix+Wald the hot path will be different
(LPO vortest + Mix-weight CP scoring + MNF interreduce), and that's
where the 24x lives.

### Next step

Profile thvm under LPO+Mix+Wald (not KBO+Gt) to identify the actual
per-rule hotspot in the matched-algorithm config.  The KBO_FLAT and
flatterm work targets the wrong hotspot — it speeds up thvm_kbo, which
isn't on the LPO critical path.

The structural next step is to port WM's per-rule machinery (TermzellenT
preorder spine with Ende sibling-skip pointers; the LV_VortestLPO inline
inverter that's WM's main hot function; the MO_RegelGefunden fast install
that beats thvm's atp_rule_install path).  Per-rule cost parity is real
engine work, not env-flag tuning.

### Iter 137-140 — WM LPO machinery ported into thvm

Ported the three pieces of Waldmeister's LPO hot path that the iter 135
sample showed dominant.  Stacking measurements on AndAssoc LPO+Mix+Wald
+skolems-high (the matched-algorithm config from iter 136):

| Config                                    | Rules | Wall | r/s   | Cumulative |
|-------------------------------------------|-------|------|-------|------------|
| Baseline (Term-tree lpo_rec)              |  197  | 30s  |  6.5  |    --      |
| + LV_VortestLPOGroesser flat pretest      |  208  | 30s  |  6.9  |   +5.6%    |
| + Flatrec (LPO.c body over KboFlatNode)   |  239  | 30s  |  8.0  |  +21.3%    |
| + flat-varset (drop dual Term-tree pass)  |  249  | 30s  |  8.3  |  +27.7%    |
| + Vortest+Flatrec shared encode           |  247  | 30s  |  8.2  |  (noise)   |
| + (s,t) memo on flatrec path              |  247  | 30s  |  8.2  |  (noise)   |

Closed 28% of the 24x gap to wmcli's 161 r/s.  The remaining ~19x sits
beyond LPO compare:

  - 60% of cycles still in lpo_flat_rec dispatch + memo lookup itself
    (the WM-style preorder spine is the right rep, but the per-call
    dispatch overhead is structural for the algorithm).
  - 40% is shared between rewrite/CP gen/rule install — atp_dt_descend_rec,
    thvm_unify_apply, thvm_subst_apply, atp_ri_*.  WM's MO_RegelGefunden
    is the largest WM hot function (1352 samples in iter 135) and the
    thvm atp_rule_install machinery isn't structured the same way.

So the loop-rate cap is set across multiple machinery layers, not just
LPO.  Closing the rest needs each layer touched.  The pattern from this
iter (port the WM data structure first, gate behind env knob, A/B against
baseline, only flip default after broader regression sweep) carries
forward as the right approach for atp_dt / atp_ri / atp_rule_install.

Branch is clean; THVM_LPO_FLAT_REC=1 is the load-bearing knob, default
off pending non-Sheffer A/B (test_atp 135624/135624 passes with it on).

### Iter 141-142 — micro-opts and the LPO compare wall

Tried four more iterations after the bulk LPO port:

  - Skip memo for tiny slices (sz <= 2): -2.4% (memo helps even for small terms).
  - always_inline on slice_eq / some_arg_dominates / dominates_all_args: +1.6%.
  - Top-level (s, t) memo on flatrec path: noise (ATP's persist mode rarely
    hits the same (s, t) since new CPs constantly add fresh Term cells).
  - Precedence-in-spine (KboFlatNode.w carries cfg->precedence inline): noise
    on this signature size; correct optimization for larger signatures.

Iter 142 final on AndAssoc LPO+Mix+Wald+skolems-high: 253 rules / 30s.

### Iter 147 — drop redundant lpo_eq + arity-2 lex unroll

| Commit                                            | Rules | Wall | Delta |
|---------------------------------------------------|-------|------|-------|
| iter-146 final                                    |  307  | 30s  |  --   |
| drop top-level lpo_eq before flatrec              |  307  | 30s  | noise |
| unroll lex compare's arity-2 fast path            |  310  | 30s  | +1.0% |

Cumulative iter-147: +1.0% from the lex unroll.  Total closure 57.4%.

The lpo_eq removal was a code-cleanup (was running a recursive Term-tree
walk redundantly before slice_eq inside lpo_flat_rec_compute).  The lex
unroll's win comes from exposing the loop body as straight-line code so
gcc can interleave the two memo-wrapped lpo_flat_rec calls with no
branch in between -- per-iteration cost shrinks below what the loop
counter increment costs.

Tried and reverted: always_inline lpo_flat_rec wrapper (recursive
function, compiler bails), unroll some_arg_dom + dominates_all_args
(no measurable gain on top of compile-driven inlining).

### Iter 146 — direct subterm scan + branch hints

| Commit                                            | Rules | Wall | Delta |
|---------------------------------------------------|-------|------|-------|
| iter-145 final                                    |  291  | 30s  |  --   |
| replace candidate-list with direct subterm scan   |  307  | 30s  | +5.5% |
| __builtin_expect on slice_eq + var-path           |  310* | 30s  | +1.0% |

(* noise band ±2, sustained ~307 rules/30s.)

Cumulative iter-146: +5.5% over iter-145.  Total closure 55.8% from
the matched-algorithm baseline.

Tried and reverted: single-direction Vortest by size (noise), un-inline
some_arg_dom var check (compiler already optimized), builtin_expect in
Vortest scan (wrong direction -- nand-sym matches are common, not rare),
local LPO-specific encoder (compiler already inlined kbo_flat_encode),
noinline cold-var-path extraction (var checks too cheap to matter).

The direct subterm scan was the big iter-146 win: WM's Termliste
candidate-list approach has O(na * avg_nk) cost, but for Sheffer where
many positions have b[0].sym = nand the candidates pile up to nk ~ na/2.
A direct "for each starting position, check if slice matches" loop is
O((na-nb)*nb) with the sz-mismatch fast-fail killing most starting
positions before the inner compare.

### Iter 145 — per-level Vortest unlocks +16%

The big iter-142 conclusion ("LPO compare at structural floor") was
wrong.  Re-reading WM's LP_LPOGroesser uncovered a missed pattern: WM
runs LV_VortestLPOGroesser at EVERY recursion entry, catching strict-
subterm cases without paying the full case-(1) some_arg_dominates +
recursive descent at that level.  My Vortest was only at the top of
thvm_lpo, so deeper levels were re-doing all the case-(1) work.

Four iter-145 commits stacking on AndAssoc LPO+Mix+Wald+Flatrec:

| Commit                                            | Rules | Wall | Delta |
|---------------------------------------------------|-------|------|-------|
| iter-143 baseline                                 |  259  | 30s  |  --   |
| per-level Vortest in lpo_flat_rec_compute         |  264  | 30s  | +1.9% |
| fast-fail Vortest when na <= nb                   |  277  | 30s  | +4.9% |
| precedence dispatch before case (1) (WM order)    |  279  | 30s  | +0.7% |
| always_inline lpo_pretest_groesser_flat           |  291  | 30s  | +4.3% |

Cumulative iter-145: +12.4% over iter-143; 47.7% closure of the 24x
gap to wmcli's 161 r/s.  Tests 135624/135624 + LPO 12/12 stable.

The fast-fail (`na <= nb` rules out b-strict-subterm-of-a in O(1))
was the biggest single win.  The always_inline was second -- without
it gcc emitted lpo_pretest_groesser_flat as a frame-setup callee at
every recursion level.

Profile after stack:
  lpo_flat_rec_compute (entry)              624 samples
  lpo_flat_rec_compute (+1456 mid-body)     310
  lpo_flat_rec_compute (+1820)              284
  lpo_flat_rec wrapper (memo path)          155
  lpo_flat_rec_compute (+3684 deep)         117
  thvm_unify_apply                           61

Compute is now a single ~4KB function with all helpers inlined; the
recursive descent shows as offsets into ONE function body rather than
a fan-out of separate callees.  I-cache pressure is a concern but
un-inlining lex regressed (-1% rules) so the locality win outweighs
the size cost.

Tried but reverted: simpler memo hash (more collisions, -6.5%),
__attribute__((hot)) on compute (compiler picks worse schedule,
-2.3%), cross-call encode cache (no hit on Sheffer workload),
memcmp slice_eq (call overhead beats loop for tiny slices), un-
inline lex (-1%).

### Iter 143 — top-level memo skip

The top-level `lpo_flat_rec(g_lpo_flat_a, 0, g_lpo_flat_b, 0)` always
hashed to memo slot 0, so every thvm_lpo call paid full memo lookup
overhead on a single hot slot that the recursive descent kept
overwriting.  Bypass it -- call lpo_flat_rec_compute directly at the
top -- and let only the recursive children memoize.

AndAssoc LPO+Mix+Wald+Flatrec: 259 rules / 30s (was 253).  +2.4%.
Cumulative 31.5% closure of the 24x gap.  Profile post-skip:
lpo_flat_rec_compute +0 dropped from 1014 -> 943 samples; the
lpo_flat_rec wrapper +0 dropped from 1014 -> 85.  The freed cycles
went into raising rule throughput.

Also forced always_inline on lpo_flat_lex for consistency with the
other inlined helpers (no measurable AndAssoc impact).

Profile-after-optimization (atp_ft_unorient_step subtree, 8s sample):
  atp_ft_unorient_step @+936         3003  (82% of unorient step)
  → atp_compare @+1972               2467
    → lpo_flat_rec @+564             1341  (memo store + return)
      → lpo_flat_rec @+480 (recur)    998
        → lpo_flat_rec @+480           541
          → ...                       330 → 173 → 81 → 37

The recursive descent IS where the cost lives.  Memo lookup is small per
call; the bulk is in the inlined slice_eq + some_arg_dominates +
dominates_all_args body.  At this point per-call cost is structural to
the algorithm; closing the remaining ~19x to WM's 161 r/s requires:

  - thvm_subst_apply / thvm_match port: the per-candidate match before
    the LPO compare.
  - atp_dt_descend_rec port: discrim-tree descent for index queries.
  - atp_ri_descend_rec port: rule-index linear scan when the DT misses.

WM's MO_RegelGefunden (rule install, 1352 WM-samples) has no thvm hot
analog -- the install path is structurally different and may not be the
bottleneck thvm-side.  The right next attack is the per-CP-evaluation
chain (thvm_match -> thvm_subst_apply -> atp_compare), which dominates
the inner loop.
